      "updated": 1793391400403,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "GPU = many SMs. SMs = several processing blocks (dispatcher + cores) \nsharing control logic and on-chip memory.\n\nLaunch grid -> its blocks are handed to SMs in any order, whole. \nThat's transparent scalability, and it costs one rule: threads\nin different blocks must not wait on each other.\n\nOn the SM a block is cut into warps of 32 consecutive threads. \nA warp runs SIMD: one instruction, 32 threads.\n\nDivergence: threads in one warp take different paths -> the processing\nblock runs the paths in passes, each thread active in its own pass.\nCost = extra passes + idle cores.\n\nThe SM holds far more warps than it runs at once. Only some issue at\nany instant; the rest wait for slow operations without stalling\nthe cores. That's latency hiding.\n\nOccupancy = warps (or threads) / max the SM can hold. Higher =\nbetter hiding.\n\nEach card has its own per-SM limits: blocks, threads, registers, shared\nmemory. For any kernel one of them runs out first and caps occupancy.\n\nTools: occupancy calculator (Nsight) and occupancy API to choose\nblock sizes; device query API to read a card's limits at runtime.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "GPU = many SMs. SMs = several processing blocks (dispatcher + cores) \nsharing control logic and on-chip memory.\n\nLaunch grid -> its blocks are handed to SMs in any order, whole. \nThat's transparent scalability, and it costs one rule: threads\nin different blocks must not wait on each other.\n\nOn the SM a block is cut into warps of 32 consecutive threads. \nA warp runs SIMD: one instruction, 32 threads.\n\nDivergence: threads in one warp take different paths -> the processing\nblock runs the paths in passes, each thread active in its own pass.\nCost = extra passes + idle cores.\n\nThe SM holds far more warps than it runs at once. Only some issue at\nany instant; the rest wait for slow operations without stalling\nthe cores. That's latency hiding.\n\nOccupancy = warps (or threads) / max the SM can hold. Higher =\nbetter hiding.\n\nEach card has its own per-SM limits: blocks, threads, registers, shared\nmemory. For any kernel one of them runs out first and caps occupancy.\n\nTools: occupancy calculator (Nsight) and occupancy API to choose\nblock sizes; device query API to read a card's limits at runtime.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
